# 📊 System Monitoring — Simple Guide

Yeh commands batati hain ke aapka Linux system (Disk, RAM, CPU) kaisa perform kar raha hai.

### 1. Disk Usage (df, du)
- **`df -h`**: Batata hai ke aapki hard drive (Disk) mein kitni jagah khali hai (Human-readable format mein).
- **`du -sh <folder>`**: Batata hai ke kisi specific folder ne kitni jagah li hui hai.

### 2. Memory Usage (free, vmstat)
- **`free -h`**: Batata hai ke aapki RAM (Memory) kitni use ho rahi hai aur kitni free hai.
- **`vmstat 1`**: Har 1 second baad memory aur CPU ki statistics dikhata hai.

### 3. CPU Usage (top, mpstat)
- **`top`** (ya `htop`): Live monitor hai jo dikhata hai kaunsa process sab se zyada CPU aur RAM kha raha hai.
- **`mpstat`**: Har ek CPU core ki alag alag usage batata hai.

### 4. Logs (/var/log, journalctl)
- **`ls /var/log`**: System aur services ki log files yahan save hoti hain (jaise `syslog`, `auth.log`).
- **`journalctl -xe`**: Systemd ke zariye system errors aur logs check karne ke liye (latest errors dikhata hai).

### 5. System Info (uname, uptime)
- **`uname -a`**: Aapke OS (Kernel) ki poori detail batata hai (jaise WSL2, Linux version).
- **`uptime`**: Batata hai ke system kitni der se on hai aur pichle 1, 5, 15 minute ka average load kya hai.

